the turbulence of the trump  administration has left a slew of former  appointees and officials in its wake  while these folks for any number of  reasons couldn't weather the storm  attorney general bill barr has hung on  taking each crisis as an opportunity to  assert himself  the president has not attempted to  interfere in these decisions  on the contrary he has told me from the  start that he expects me to exercise my  independent judgment to make whatever  call i think is right and that is  precisely what i've done  he saw an opportunity to come back into  a position where he would have a lot of  power  a lot of sway within a chaotic  administration  he had an agenda that he wanted to carry  forward from his time as previous  attorney general  what remains to be seen with criticism  and controversy reaching a fever pitch  is how it will all turn out will his  fervent support of executive power  buttress further presidents and  attorneys general for years to  come or will he for the second time in  his life  hit a dead end and be jettisoned from  office along with the other members of  another one-term presidency  william barr was born in 1950 to parents  donald and mary margaret  both professors at columbia university  after going to elite schools corpus  christi and horace mann  bill attended columbia himself where he  got his bachelor's and master's degrees  in 1989 he was appointed by george h.w  bush to the office of legal counsel  where he began writing opinions  supporting broad powers for the  president and the executive branch  including for the u.s invasion of panama  and subsequent arrest of manuel noriega  in 1991 barr was promoted from deputy  attorney general to acting attorney  general  a few weeks after that president bush  officially nominated barr to the  position of aj  barr sailed to confirmation with  bipartisan support  including that of someone who would call  for his resignation  28 years later but i personally like you  i think you're a heck of an honorable  guy  i think you're press someone i can work  with and you've demonstrated that  when i say can work with i don't mean  you do it our way i mean you  when you sit down and negotiate you  genuinely negotiate  the new york times in 1992 described the  central theme of his tenure  as a contention that violent crime can  be reduced by expanding federal and  state prisons  to jail habitual offenders to this end  he authored a report  the case for more incarceration a study  in 1999 called it overly simplistic  and a university of minnesota  criminologist has said its data is  deceptively presented however barr is  far from the only public servant  responsible for the growth in  incarceration since his time in office  the u.s prison population has about  doubled and the u.s has the most  incarcerated people of any country  in the world after finishing his first  run as attorney  general he spent two and a half decades  in the private sector  working for a phone company that you may  have heard of can you hear me now  good he worked for  verizon and its predecessor company gte  where he was general counsel and he  spent about 15 years at those companies  as they effectively consolidated the  telecommunications industry he is fairly  unique  for an attorney general because  he came to the job with a pretty  extensive  background in corporate america  cut to early 2019 after driving jeff  sessions out as attorney general and the  mueller investigation soon coming to a  conclusion  trump figured he could use a strong  advocate i think that  the the probe is a disaster for our  country  when trump fired sessions there was a  small group of  establishment conservatives  establishment republicans who  took it upon themselves to go and find  somebody who could be  an acceptable attorney general barr had  already  gotten on the radar of trump in the  white house because  he wrote an unsolicited letter  criticizing the mueller investigation  well before he was considered to be you  know for the attorney general position  but why decades later did he want the ag  job again  i'm almost 70 years old i was almost 70  years old and slipping happily into  retirement  i had nothing to prove and i had no  desire to return to government  he saw an opportunity to  re-establish the power of the executive  branch  bars justice department you know has  played a central role  in uh defending the administration and  defending the white house  i don't think that his methodology and  beliefs have changed  so much as his actions have changed  under the bush administration in in the  early 90s  there was a much more of an  institutional framework  and a policy making framework under  which  barr operated and was and was  constrained trains kind of ran on time  under the trump administration a lot of  that has disintegrated it's a lot of  chaos  and now he has much more uh leeway  in terms of what he can do bar was  confirmed  this time on a near party line vote in  february of 2019  a month later he set the tone for his  time in office  releasing a four-page letter on the  mueller report  investigation did not establish that  members of the trump campaign conspired  or coordinated  with the russian government in its bar  mischaracterized  the findings of the muller report it was  a four-page summary  the mauler report was 500 pages with  detail after detail  of interactions and contacts between the  trump campaign  and russians and the mueller report also  concluded that the trump campaign  actively sought out russian assistance  and welcomed russian assistance  and the the muller report also found  that there were at least 10  examples where trump obstructed justice  now barr didn't  provide any of this information in his  summary  and barr put his summary out about a  month before the mueller report became  public  even mueller himself wrote bar a letter  saying that he mischaracterized  mueller's findings  it was the greatest hoax ever  perpetrated on the people of this  country  and you know what i am so proud of our  attorney general  that he is looking into it i think it's  great in the aftermath  barr also ordered that charges against  michael flynn be dropped  and that the recommended sentence for  roger stone be reduced  trump later commuted his sentence anyway  in more recent days  barr has used the power of his office in  more forceful ways  in early june he ordered the removal of  protesters near the white house  leading to acts of violence against what  was a largely peaceful crowd  president trump was subsequently able to  conduct this now famous photo op  then about a month later i'm just here  to do my job  barr also unceremoniously attempted to  oust manhattan u.s attorney jeffrey  berman  barr just announced that berman was  going to step down and  hours later berman issued a statement  saying he wasn't going to step down  next day barr said that he had talked to  trump and berman was going to be fired  and berman  at that point negotiated a situation in  which his deputy  would take over leading the the u.s  attorney's office  the reason why this is so controversial  is because that u.s attorney's office  has been  investigating actions by trump and and  actions by trump's associates  as we look to the future barr looks to  make more waves that benefit the man who  appointed him  one of the more amusing aspects of  modern progressive  polemic is their breathless attacks on  the  unitary executive theory  immediately after president trump won  election opponents inaugurated what they  called  the resistance and they rallied around  an explicit strategy of using every tool  and maneuver to sabotage the functioning  of the executive branch and his  administration  a number of the cases that barr has  personally involved himself in  are also cases that trump wants to  have his attorney general be involved in  now barr will say that that's just doing  impartial justice in terms of  approaching these different cases  the biggest investigation that barr  ordered is a investigation into  the fbi's uh probe into trump's campaign  and uh and trump associates uh related  to russian interference in the 2016  election now that's interesting because  then that puts it right as the election  this year is heating up we potentially  have a situation  where bar and the justice department are  going to start to roll out  uh findings that um undercut  things that were done by the obama  administration and by virtue things that  were done  under the obama biden administration and  it'll be stuff that  trump and his conservative allies most  likely will seize on and use to their  advantage  [Music]  you

